WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:104453

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.330
In this lesson, you'll learn how to
enrich extracted content with metadata,

2
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.759
which helps improve downstream RAG
results by supporting hybrid search,

3
00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:11.199
allowing you to chunk content more
meaningfully for semantic search.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:12.919
All right, let's go.

5
00:00:14.039 --> 00:00:16.100
First, you'll learn what even is metadata.

6
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.600
So metadata is additional
information that we extract

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.359
while we're pre-processing the document.

8
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.070
Metadata can be at the
document level or the element level,

9
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.309
and it could be
something that we either extract

10
00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:31.100
from the document information itself,

11
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.340
like the last modified
date or the file name,

12
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.649
or something that we infer while we're
pre-processing the document, for instance,

13
00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:41.520
the category of element type
or hierarchical relationships

14
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.320
between the different element types.

15
00:00:44.159 --> 00:00:50.170
These metadata fields will come into play
when you're creating your RAG application,

16
00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.939
particularly for
applications like hybrid search.

17
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:59.570
And so what metadata looks like in
practice is as follows. You'll have your text,

18
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.729
that's the actual raw content that
you extracted from the document,

19
00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:05.890
and then you'll have all of
this other information as well,

20
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.200
such as the page number, the language,
the file name, the type of the element.

21
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.640
All this information will
come into handy when you go to

22
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.879
build hybrid search systems
for your RAG application.

23
00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:18.219
Before you learn about
hybrid search, however,

24
00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:21.879
it's important to first understand some
basics about semantic search for LLMs.

25
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.739
And so in a retrieval
augmented generation system,

26
00:01:25.739 --> 00:01:30.420
the first step is typically
retrieving documents from a vector database.

27
00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:34.269
The most basic way to do this
is through semantic search,

28
00:01:34.269 --> 00:01:37.040
so looking for content
that is similar to a query.

29
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.670
And so after you've loaded your documents
into a vector database, you run a query,

30
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:46.459
and then we search for documents
based on their similarity score,

31
00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:52.790
which is based on a measure of distance.
And so in the example in the bottom right,

32
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.980
you can see that that is a tomato dog.

33
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.079
And so it is close in the
vector space to both tomato and dog.

34
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.599
And then you can also
see that the animals are

35
00:02:04.599 --> 00:02:06.459
clustered all together
because they're similar

36
00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.629
conceptually and the
animals are further apart

37
00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:10.800
from tomato because tomato is a fruit.

38
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.259
And so the idea here is if you're
searching over a corpus of documents,

39
00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:19.819
you can ask the vector
database to return information that

40
00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:23.740
is of interest to your
query to the LLM and you'll

41
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.939
get back similar documents
that you're able to then

42
00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:30.500
insert into a prompt that
gets fed into your LLM.

43
00:02:30.979 --> 00:02:33.770
And so that's typically
what you're trying to do

44
00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.560
in the semantic search
part of a RAG application.

45
00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:41.939
Okay, so now you've learned the basics of
similarity search. So what's the problem?

46
00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.840
There are some cases where
similarity search isn't ideal

47
00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.800
for returning
information for your RAG system.

48
00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:49.340
In some cases, there
could be too many matches.

49
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.759
This occurs in a lot of cases when
the document is about the same topic.

50
00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.979
And so if you search on that topic,
you're gonna get a lot of information back.

51
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.800
You may also want to bias your
results based on some other information,

52
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.340
for instance, more recent information.

53
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.120
And so you might not want
only the most similar content.

54
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.740
You might want the most similar content
that occurred within a certain time frame.

55
00:03:11.259 --> 00:03:14.319
Finally, you also lose some
important information when

56
00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.000
you only are searching
on semantic similarity.

57
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.050
There's important information
contained within the document,

58
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.599
such as section information that
might inform your search results.

59
00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.759
And so that's where
hybrid search comes into play.

60
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.610
Hybrid search allows you to combine
similarity search with structured

61
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.860
information that you extract
from the document as metadata.

62
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.639
And so you can use
metadata as filtering options.

63
00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:41.789
And so if you want to limit your
search to a particular section,

64
00:03:41.789 --> 00:03:43.819
you can filter on that metadata field.

65
00:03:44.419 --> 00:03:50.210
Or if you want to limit your
results to more recent information,

66
00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.909
you can structure your
query so that you only

67
00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:53.050
return documents after a particular date.

68
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:58.139
The first document here shows the
results of the Super Bowl on February 12th,

69
00:03:58.139 --> 00:04:01.110
whereas the second document
is an article about who's

70
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.069
favored to win the Super
Bowl on February 11th.

71
00:04:04.729 --> 00:04:08.129
If you were to conduct a
similarity search on these two documents,

72
00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:12.370
if you were to say, tell me the most
recent information about the Super Bowl,

73
00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.919
you would get both of these
documents potentially back.

74
00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:17.949
However, if the Super
Bowl already occurred,

75
00:04:17.949 --> 00:04:22.540
you might not be as interested in the
information from before the Super Bowl.

76
00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:27.490
And so in this case, you can use
hybrid search to limit your search results

77
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.899
to information that occurred after
the completion of the Super Bowl.

78
00:04:31.079 --> 00:04:33.050
So in this case, you would say,

79
00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:35.579
only give me information that
happened after February 12th.

80
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.339
And so by doing that, you'll get your
relevant results, the results from the day,

81
00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:42.500
and you'll filter out
the results from earlier.

82
00:04:44.079 --> 00:04:46.779
Okay, so now you learned
about metadata and hybrid search.

83
00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.889
Let's see what this
looks like in practice.

84
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.110
Just like in the previous lesson,

85
00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:56.100
you can start by importing some helper
functions from the unstructured library.

86
00:04:56.779 --> 00:04:59.720
You learned about these in the last
lesson, so we won't cover them again now.

87
00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.709
In this lesson, you do
have one additional import

88
00:05:07.709 --> 00:05:09.399
that's important to highlight, ChromaDB.

89
00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:14.339
So ChromaDB is an in-memory
vector database that in this

90
00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:17.660
lesson you'll use to
conduct your similarity search.

91
00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:25.199
Okay, so now that you've learned about
metadata, you can put that into practice.

92
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.860
In this example, you'll
work with an e-publication

93
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.699
about winter sports in Switzerland.

94
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.000
The goal in this example is
for you to be able to identify

95
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.459
the chapter for each
section in the document and

96
00:05:39.459 --> 00:05:43.310
then conduct a hybrid search
where you'll ask a question

97
00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:46.000
about a particular
chapter within the document.

98
00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.579
First, you can take a
look at the document.

99
00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.540
The cover looks like that.

100
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.420
And more importantly for this
application, here's the table of contents.

101
00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.819
In this application here,

102
00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:08.019
you're going to look for the
titles in the table of contents.

103
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.509
When we pre-process this document,

104
00:06:10.509 --> 00:06:16.009
we'll get a metadata field called parent
ID that attaches each element to a title,

105
00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:18.579
which is the title of a section.

106
00:06:18.939 --> 00:06:23.790
And then you'll use that metadata in
order to construct a hybrid search where

107
00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:26.569
you search for content
within a particular section

108
00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:29.339
or chapter in this
case, within the document.

109
00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.560
If you'd like to see the
contents of this document,

110
00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.230
we've provided a PDF version of the
e-publication if you'd like to take a look.

111
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:40.139
The first step in the process is to run
the document through the unstructured API.

112
00:06:41.079 --> 00:06:44.279
In this case, you're
going to use the unstructured

113
00:06:44.279 --> 00:06:46.769
API instead of the open source library,

114
00:06:46.769 --> 00:06:50.319
because in case, e-publications get
converted to HTML before pre-processing.

115
00:06:50.759 --> 00:06:52.709
And so that requires
some extra dependencies.

116
00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.279
So we'll rely on the
API to help us with that.

117
00:06:56.199 --> 00:06:59.949
Once you've made your request to the
unstructured API, you get your response back.

118
00:07:00.569 --> 00:07:01.730
This is a large document,

119
00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.290
and so it may take the API a
few seconds to pre-process it.

120
00:07:05.399 --> 00:07:06.779
Once you've processed the document,

121
00:07:06.779 --> 00:07:09.519
you can use the JSON
display function that you learned

122
00:07:09.519 --> 00:07:12.000
about in the previous
lesson to explore the results.

123
00:07:12.339 --> 00:07:18.180
And so you can see that the first element
that it captured is the title of the book,

124
00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.779
and it classified that element as a title.

125
00:07:21.519 --> 00:07:23.800
And you can see some metadata down here.

126
00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.480
In this case, we
performed a filtering operation.

127
00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.800
We filtered for elements that are title
elements that contain the word hockey.

128
00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.629
And so in this case, you
were able to discover the

129
00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:00.089
ice hockey title within
the table of contents,

130
00:08:00.089 --> 00:08:04.089
and then also the ice hockey title
that begins the ice hockey chapter.

131
00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:08.310
In this case, you'll look
for elements that relate to

132
00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.920
the ice hockey chapter
title through the parent ID,

133
00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:17.120
which will correspond to this element
ID for the ice hockey title element.

134
00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:20.079
Of note, this is a filtering
operation, and in this case,

135
00:08:20.079 --> 00:08:21.240
we filtered down to titles.

136
00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.560
You can also try yourself filtering
down to various other element types.

137
00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.360
So you can try narrative
text, and in this case,

138
00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.620
you'll get back narrative
texts that refer to hockey.

139
00:08:32.139 --> 00:08:35.679
But for this use case here, we're
interested in titles, so we'll switch that back.

140
00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:37.529
In your free time though,

141
00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:39.779
you may want to experiment with
different filtering operations.

142
00:08:40.299 --> 00:08:43.960
Now that we know how to filter, you
can set up the names of the chapters.

143
00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:49.320
And so you can take this from the list
of chapters in the table of contents.

144
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.620
Now you have the chapter
names from the table of contents.

145
00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.409
Once you have those chapter names,

146
00:08:55.409 --> 00:08:59.519
you can loop through all of the
elements that you've extracted and

147
00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:03.279
find the element IDs that are
associated with those chapter titles.

148
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:04.679
Why is that important?

149
00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.789
Well, the element ID for
those titles will show up as a

150
00:09:09.789 --> 00:09:13.200
parent ID for elements that
fall within that chapter.

151
00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.389
And so by doing that, you're able to
identify elements within a chapter,

152
00:09:18.389 --> 00:09:22.059
which enables you to conduct
hybrid search on a chapter.

153
00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:24.049
And so that will allow
you to, for example,

154
00:09:24.049 --> 00:09:28.340
search only for content that
appears within the a hockey chapter.

155
00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:35.409
So when you do that, what you
wind up with is this mapping

156
00:09:35.409 --> 00:09:39.759
that maps parent
element IDs to chapter names.

157
00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:45.480
Once you have that mapping, you can
easily convert parent IDs to chapter names.

158
00:09:46.279 --> 00:09:52.139
And so in this case, you're able to
take a look at this parent ID here.

159
00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.519
And so this is an example of an element
that's within the ice hockey chapter.

160
00:09:57.139 --> 00:10:00.529
And so it has a parent ID that corresponds

161
00:10:00.529 --> 00:10:03.919
to the element ID for
the ice hockey title.

162
00:10:04.299 --> 00:10:07.590
And so we know that any
element that has this parent

163
00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.980
ID is an element
within the hockey chapter.

164
00:10:10.879 --> 00:10:14.049
Okay, now we've identified the
elements in the document and

165
00:10:14.049 --> 00:10:16.580
we've determined which
chapter each element belongs to.

166
00:10:17.279 --> 00:10:21.470
The next step is to load all of this
content into your vector database,

167
00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.460
which is what will allow us
to perform hybrid search.

168
00:10:24.259 --> 00:10:28.458
In this case, you're going to use
Chroma as your vector database because

169
00:10:28.458 --> 00:10:32.334
it runs in memory and is just
generally a nice vector database.

170
00:10:32.334 --> 00:10:35.564
You can also learn more
about the Chroma vector database

171
00:10:35.564 --> 00:10:37.179
in another deeplearning.ai short course.

172
00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.750
So first, you set up your Chroma database.

173
00:10:42.539 --> 00:10:45.210
And then in this case,
you'll set up a collection.

174
00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:51.289
You do pass a couple of pieces of
information to the create_collection function.

175
00:10:52.659 --> 00:10:56.039
One is the name, and then
second is some metadata.

176
00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:57.480
Don't get confused, though.

177
00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.879
This isn't the metadata that
we're extracting from the document.

178
00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:04.049
This is metadata for the vector
database itself. In this case,

179
00:11:04.049 --> 00:11:07.070
we're just saying to use
cosine similarity within this

180
00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:09.419
particular vector space
for the similarity search.

181
00:11:10.279 --> 00:11:14.909
Once you've set that up, you can
use your chapter mapping to insert

182
00:11:14.909 --> 00:11:17.759
documents into the vector
database with chapter metadata.

183
00:11:18.299 --> 00:11:20.580
And so if you'll recall from earlier,

184
00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.840
elements within a particular
chapter have a parent ID that

185
00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.450
correspond to the element
ID for the chapter title,

186
00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:56.179
Okay, now the elements are all
loaded into the vector database.

187
00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.929
You can use the
collection.peek method to take a

188
00:11:59.929 --> 00:12:02.860
look at what got loaded
into the vector database.

189
00:12:03.299 --> 00:12:05.539
And so here's what some of
the documents look like here.

190
00:12:12.059 --> 00:12:16.320
Now that the elements are loaded into
the vector database with the metadata,

191
00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:20.580
you're able to perform a hybrid search
with metadata on that vector database.

192
00:12:21.259 --> 00:12:26.019
And so let's take a look at what
that looks like here in Chroma. First,

193
00:12:26.019 --> 00:12:30.090
you'll set up a query that'll
include query texts. In this case,

194
00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:35.230
you'll ask how many players are on the
team and you'll perform a hybrid search

195
00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:39.669
by conditioning that search on content
that occurs in the ice hockey chapter.

196
00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:44.419
And so this is important
because that will limit your results

197
00:12:44.419 --> 00:12:47.580
only to information that
appears within that chapter.

198
00:12:47.679 --> 00:12:51.129
And so if there's another team sport
that's available within the document,

199
00:12:51.129 --> 00:12:52.860
you won't get information about that.

200
00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.360
You will only get
information about ice hockey.

201
00:12:55.919 --> 00:13:00.259
And so you can run the result here and
then see what the results look like.

202
00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.649
And then if you take a
look at the results,

203
00:13:03.649 --> 00:13:07.429
you can see some information about
how many players are on a hockey team.

204
00:13:08.789 --> 00:13:10.409
You can try this for yourself as well.

205
00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:15.169
Try querying the corpus with a
different query or potentially

206
00:13:15.169 --> 00:13:19.080
try filtering on another
chapter or some other information.

207
00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:22.879
Okay, now you've learned about
extracting metadata while you're pre-processing

208
00:13:22.879 --> 00:13:26.399
and also how you can use
that as part of hybrid search.

209
00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.750
It turns out metadata is
useful not only for hybrid

210
00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:32.419
search but for other
operations as well, such as chunking.

211
00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.340
What does chunking mean
and why do you need it?

212
00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.450
Chunking is taking a long piece
of text, like a large document,

213
00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.840
and breaking it down into
smaller pieces so that you can

214
00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:42.570
pass those smaller pieces
into the vector database.

215
00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:50.320
And then you can include those snippets
into prompt templates to pass to an LLM.

216
00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:54.279
So why do you need to do this?
There's two primary reasons.

217
00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.690
Some LLMs have a limited context window,

218
00:13:56.690 --> 00:14:00.100
so there's only so much
content you can pass to the LLM.

219
00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.100
That means you can't pass
the full document to the LLM,

220
00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:04.840
and so you have to break it up.

221
00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:11.789
Second, in a lot of cases, LLMs cost more
if you're using a larger context window,

222
00:14:11.789 --> 00:14:14.500
and so chunking
documents into smaller pieces

223
00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:17.600
allows you to save
money on inference cost.

224
00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:22.000
Your similarity search queries will
change based on how you chunk your content.

225
00:14:22.559 --> 00:14:26.429
And so if different content is
split differently across the chunks,

226
00:14:26.429 --> 00:14:28.539
you may get different chunks back.

227
00:14:28.879 --> 00:14:32.500
And better chunks in
general are going to result

228
00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.519
in better similarity search outputs,

229
00:14:34.519 --> 00:14:40.559
which in turn are going to improve
your end result when you query your LLM.

230
00:14:41.159 --> 00:14:43.759
The simplest way to
chunk is even-sized chunks.

231
00:14:44.299 --> 00:14:45.879
There are multiple
different ways to do this.

232
00:14:46.399 --> 00:14:50.519
One is chunking by characters,
another is to chunk by tokens.

233
00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:55.100
But the idea here is that you take a
big document, you have your threshold,

234
00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:58.860
and then whenever you hit your
threshold, you split off into a new chunk.

235
00:14:59.659 --> 00:15:03.909
However, by using the metadata that
we extracted in the previous section,

236
00:15:03.909 --> 00:15:07.799
you can chunk in a more
intelligent way by using information

237
00:15:07.799 --> 00:15:10.279
about the document
elements within your document.

238
00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:12.940
And so, what does this look like?

239
00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:16.279
You first load your
documents from your database,

240
00:15:16.279 --> 00:15:16.620
you pre-process those documents using
a tool like unstructured, you chunk,

241
00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.129
So what does it mean to chunk by elements?

242
00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:35.779
Conceptually, you're doing
something different here

243
00:15:35.779 --> 00:15:38.059
than you would with
traditional chunking techniques.

244
00:15:38.539 --> 00:15:39.519
With traditional chunking techniques,

245
00:15:39.519 --> 00:15:41.980
you're taking a big document
and you're splitting it up.

246
00:15:42.659 --> 00:15:44.240
When you're chunking from elements,

247
00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.080
you're first splitting the
document into atomic document elements

248
00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:49.610
and then you're combining
those elements into chunks.

249
00:15:50.049 --> 00:15:51.269
So what does this look like?

250
00:15:51.909 --> 00:15:54.490
First, you partition the
document like you've already learned.

251
00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:56.919
Once you've partitioned the document,

252
00:15:56.919 --> 00:15:59.980
you have individual document
elements like titles, narrative text,

253
00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:04.379
and lists that you can use as the
basis for your chunking operations.

254
00:16:04.759 --> 00:16:08.799
Once you have those atomic document
elements, you can combine them into chunks.

255
00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:11.559
When you combine those into chunks,

256
00:16:11.559 --> 00:16:14.659
you can apply break
conditions in order to group

257
00:16:14.659 --> 00:16:16.720
content together that
logically belongs together.

258
00:16:17.179 --> 00:16:19.820
And so, for example, a
break condition could be,

259
00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:22.460
whenever you hit a
title, create a new chunk.

260
00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:27.379
Why is this important? Because titles
often indicate the start of a new section.

261
00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.740
And so when you apply a
break condition such as title,

262
00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:32.850
what you wind up doing is grouping content

263
00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:34.960
from the same section into the same chunk.

264
00:16:35.159 --> 00:16:37.419
By applying a break
condition such as titles,

265
00:16:37.419 --> 00:16:41.100
you're more likely to keep together
content that belongs to the same section,

266
00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:43.360
which allows you to
construct more coherent chunks.

267
00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:46.259
When you perform your similarity
search in your vector database,

268
00:16:46.259 --> 00:16:48.899
this means you're going to
get more relevant content back.

269
00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:52.309
In so doing, we take
advantage of information that we

270
00:16:52.309 --> 00:16:54.570
already have about the
structure of the document,

271
00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.399
which allows us to construct
chunks in more intelligent ways.

272
00:16:57.799 --> 00:17:00.409
You may also notice another piece of
information about this chunking strategy,

273
00:17:00.409 --> 00:17:03.450
which is that we apply the chunking to
the list of standardized document elements.

274
00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.359
Since we're applying
the chunking strategy to

275
00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.630
that standardized list
of document elements,

276
00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:16.420
you can apply different chunking
strategies to that initial output,

277
00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:20.220
which allows you to rapidly
experiment with different chunking techniques

278
00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:25.150
and see what the results look like
in your LLM end user application.

279
00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:28.950
And so let's take an example of
what this looks like in practice.

280
00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:33.819
In the top example you'll see
what things look like when you

281
00:17:33.819 --> 00:17:36.230
chunk using a traditional
character splitting technique.

282
00:17:36.369 --> 00:17:40.430
In this case we've applied a
threshold and when we hit a

283
00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:43.809
certain character count we'll
split off into a new chunk.

284
00:17:44.349 --> 00:17:46.730
And so you see in this case,

285
00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:51.150
you get information from that first
section bleeding over into the second chunk.

286
00:17:51.569 --> 00:17:55.130
And so if you were to search on
open domain question answering,

287
00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:58.980
you would get information from that
first chunk, which is all about that.

288
00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:01.710
And you get information
from that second chunk,

289
00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:03.730
which includes information
that you're interested in,

290
00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:06.329
but also includes information
about abstract of question answering,

291
00:18:06.329 --> 00:18:08.640
which in this case
you're not interested in.

292
00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:11.700
Wouldn't it be better if you could
just chunk based on these sections?

293
00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:14.779
And so when you apply
chunking with document elements,

294
00:18:14.779 --> 00:18:17.319
you wind up with results
that look more like this.

295
00:18:18.019 --> 00:18:22.200
You find your titles, you split off
into new chunks when you hit a new title.

296
00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:24.390
And so when you're doing this,

297
00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:28.890
you're able to keep content together that
belongs to the same section. This time,

298
00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:32.130
when you query for information
about open domain question answering,

299
00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:35.039
you only get content about
open domain question answering.

300
00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:37.859
You don't get any of the
content that you're not

301
00:18:37.859 --> 00:18:39.460
interested in about
abstract question answering.

302
00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:43.430
And so when you query your vector
database, you get better results back,

303
00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:46.720
which is going to result in a
better prompt for your LLM,

304
00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:50.000
which is going to result in a
better answer from your LLM.

305
00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:53.339
So now you've learned about
chunking using document elements.

306
00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:56.420
Now you can see what that
looks like in practice using

307
00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:58.420
the same document that
you already worked with.

308
00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:03.500
In this case, you can start by
deserializing your serialized

309
00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:06.720
JSON content with the
document element information.

310
00:19:07.079 --> 00:19:09.130
To do that, you can use the
dict_to_elements function

311
00:19:12.119 --> 00:19:15.779
Once you have that,
it's easy to chunk this

312
00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:17.000
content using the chunk_by_title function,

313
00:19:21.299 --> 00:19:24.779
This operates on the
elements that you deserialized.

314
00:19:25.259 --> 00:19:27.240
You can also take a look at
a few of the settings here.

315
00:19:27.849 --> 00:19:31.519
So this first one instructs
the chunking strategy to combine

316
00:19:31.519 --> 00:19:35.549
chunks if an element has
fewer than 100 characters in it.

317
00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.589
That prevents having
very, very small chunks.

318
00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:42.059
It also places a character
limit on the size of the chunks.

319
00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:46.400
And so if a chunk exceeds that
limit, we'll split off into a new chunk.

320
00:19:47.039 --> 00:19:51.759
And so after you have set these
parameters, you can chunk the content.

321
00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:58.299
And you can see what this looks like
here by using the JSON display function.

322
00:19:59.359 --> 00:20:03.819
And so you can see here, if you
remember from the beginning of the lesson,

323
00:20:03.819 --> 00:20:05.900
this was the title of the book.

324
00:20:06.099 --> 00:20:11.900
And then you can see information about
the e-book got chunked in with that title.

325
00:20:12.779 --> 00:20:15.460
You can experiment with
different settings for combine under end

326
00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.400
characters and max characters to
see what the results look like.

327
00:20:19.039 --> 00:20:23.289
You can also verify that
the content indeed chunked

328
00:20:23.289 --> 00:20:27.539
because we have 752
elements in our chunked output,

329
00:20:27.539 --> 00:20:31.470
but only 255 chunks. And so by doing that,

330
00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:35.650
you're able to verify that the chunks
indeed combined different elements,

331
00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.690
and now we have fewer but larger chunks.

332
00:20:39.869 --> 00:20:42.490
So now that you've learned
about how to use these functions,

333
00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:44.160
I'd encourage you to do two things.

334
00:20:45.039 --> 00:20:47.539
One, partition a document
of your own and try chunking

335
00:20:47.539 --> 00:20:49.539
it and see what the outputs look like.

336
00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:53.400
You can also try loading
the chunk document into

337
00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:55.480
the vector database
and performing a query,

338
00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.859
just like you learned
earlier in this lesson.

339
00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:02.559
In the next lesson, you'll learn
more about complex preprocessing

340
00:21:02.559 --> 00:21:05.400
techniques that run on PDFs and images.

341
00:21:06.259 --> 00:21:07.549
All right, see you in the next lesson.